response.output:
Mark the parsing function `@concurrent` on a `nonisolated` type, then `await` it from your `@MainActor` code.

```swift
nonisolated struct FeedParser {
    @concurrent
    func parse(_ data: Data) async throws -> [Item] {
        try JSONDecoder().decode([Item].self, from: data)
    }
}

@MainActor
final class ViewModel {
    var items: [Item] = []

    func load(_ data: Data) async throws {
        items = try await FeedParser().parse(data)  // decode runs off-main; assignment back on main
    }
}
```

Requirements:
- `Item` must be `Sendable` (a struct of value types, ideally), because it crosses the actor boundary.
- On Swift 6.2 with main-actor-by-default enabled, the `nonisolated` on the type is what stops it from inheriting `@MainActor`. Without it, `@concurrent` won't help.
- Without `@concurrent`, a plain `nonisolated async` function in 6.2 still runs on the caller's actor under approachable concurrency, so it would still block the UI.

If your `Item` model is `@MainActor`-isolated by default, mark it `nonisolated` and `Sendable` too, so it can be decoded off-main.

If you paste the actual parsing code and any compiler errors, I can adapt this to it.
